---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const detached = assertion('four-houses-in-five-are-one-building')
const mobile = assertion('more-mobile-homes-than-flats-in-large-buildings')
const wheremobile = assertion('where-the-mobile-homes-are')
const emptymobile = assertion('one-mobile-home-in-six-stands-empty')
const rentinghouse = assertion('renting-here-means-renting-a-house')
const condo = assertion('there-is-no-condominium-market-here')
const plumbing = assertion('a-third-of-the-empty-ones-have-no-plumbing')
const kitchens = assertion('the-kitchens-say-the-same-thing')
const lima = assertion('in-lima-a-third-and-a-bit-more')
const renterkitchen = assertion('a-renter-here-lacks-a-kitchen')
const gas = assertion('bottled-gas-is-the-map-of-the-gas-main')
const car = assertion('three-thousand-households-with-no-car')
const crowding = assertion('crowded-for-ohio-and-uncrowded-for-america')
const seven = assertion('the-same-seven-questions-and-two-answers')
const nomarket = assertion('three-quarters-of-the-empty-ones-are-on-no-market')
const ordinary = assertion('ordinary-by-one-instrument-and-not-by-the-other')
const lost = assertion('lima-has-lost-one-housing-unit-in-seven')
const owners = assertion('the-city-was-still-a-city-of-owners-in-1990')
const then = assertion('almost-nothing-lacked-plumbing-in-1990')
const stayed = assertion('five-thousand-six-hundred-households-have-not-moved-since-1989')
const allocated = assertion('a-quarter-of-the-1990-answers-were-allocated')

const spec = (a: typeof plumbing, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const plumbingSpec = spec(plumbing, 'Housing units')
const sevenSpec = spec(seven, 'Vacant housing units')
const gasSpec = spec(gas, 'Per cent of occupied homes')
const carSpec = spec(car, 'Per cent of households')
const lostSpec = spec(lost, 'Housing units in Lima')
---

<Article slug="a-third-of-the-empty-ones">
  <p>
    The site already knows a good deal about Allen County's housing: who owns it, what it costs, who
    is refused a mortgage, who is evicted, and how many units stand empty. What none of that says is
    what the buildings actually are — how many rooms, what heats them, whether the water works. The
    American Community Survey publishes fourteen tables that answer exactly those questions, and the
    1990 census published two volumes of them for a year in which nobody here had asked.
  </p>

  <h2>A third of the empty ones</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={plumbing} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="plumbing"
    title="Housing units lacking complete plumbing facilities, Allen County"
    caption="Two tables of one survey, differing only in universe. The survey publishes the first two bars and not the third; it is their difference."
    columns={['Universe', 'Units']}
    rows={plumbing.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="plumbing" set:html={JSON.stringify(plumbingSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Complete plumbing means hot and cold piped water, a flush toilet and a bath or shower. A hundred
    and one households in this county live without one of those, which is a small and serious
    number. The other twelve hundred are in houses nobody lives in — and that is a different fact,
    about a stock of buildings rather than about anybody's life.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={kitchens} />
  <p>
    Two amenities measured independently, agreeing to within four points, is the check that makes
    the subtraction worth trusting. About a third of Allen County's empty housing is not habitable
    as it stands.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lima} />

  <h2>What an interviewer wrote down</h2>
  <p>
    There is a second way to describe an empty house, which is to ask what it is empty for. Both the
    census and the survey do it, with the same seven categories in the same order, and they do not
    agree.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={seven} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="seven"
    title="Two federal instruments on the same empty houses"
    caption="The census counted on one day, 1 April 2020. The survey interviewed across sixty months, 2019 to 2023. The totals are 3,628 and 3,769."
    columns={['Category and source', 'Units']}
    rows={seven.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="seven" set:html={JSON.stringify(sevenSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Neither is wrong. A house with a sign in the yard on the first of April is <em>for rent</em>;
    averaged over five years in a county with a thin rental market, the same house is mostly not
    offered to anybody. The residual class absorbs the whole of the difference, which is what a
    residual class does — and it is the class everyone wants, because it is the nearest either file
    comes to counting abandoned houses.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={nomarket} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ordinary} />
  <p>
    A vacancy reason is an observation. A missing bathroom is not.
  </p>

  <h2>What the buildings are</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={detached} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={rentinghouse} />
  <p>
    That reverses the usual picture of a renter here. Half of them are not in a block of flats; they
    are in a house with a yard, rented from whoever owns it. The other side of the same fact is that
    almost nobody here owns anything but a house.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={condo} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={mobile} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={wheremobile} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={emptymobile} />

  <h2>What is in them</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={gas} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="gas"
    title="Homes heated with bottled, tank or LP gas"
    caption="A pipeline is a fixed asset with a service area. Every home outside it that wants gas buys it by the tank."
    columns={['Geography', 'Per cent']}
    rows={gas.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="gas" set:html={JSON.stringify(gasSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Sugar Creek Township is at fifty-six per cent and Jackson at forty-five; American Township, which
    wraps the city, is at two and a half. The gas main is a map, and this is it drawn from the
    inside of people's houses. Coal, which every county history here treats as the fuel of the
    place, is zero to within a margin of thirty homes.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={car} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="car"
    title="Households with no vehicle available"
    caption="Allen County ranks nineteenth of Ohio's eighty-eight counties on this. The county has 2,225.6 miles of road."
    columns={['Group', 'Per cent']}
    rows={car.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="car" set:html={JSON.stringify(carSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={renterkitchen} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={crowding} />

  <h2>Nineteen ninety</h2>
  <p>
    The 1990 census printed two volumes about Ohio's houses rather than its households, and each
    gives every county a single summary line. Allen County's two lines are twenty-eight numbers for
    a year the record here held nothing but counts for.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lost} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="lost"
    title="Housing units in Lima"
    caption="Four counts: the 1990 census, the 2010 and 2020 censuses, and the 2019–2023 survey. The county's own total rose over the same span."
    columns={['Year', 'Units']}
    rows={lost.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="lost" set:html={JSON.stringify(lostSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={owners} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={then} />
  <p>
    That is the finding this piece began with, seen from the other end. The share of Allen County's
    housing without working plumbing has risen roughly sevenfold in thirty-three years, on a stock
    that barely grew, and almost none of the increase is in a house anybody lives in.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={stayed} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={allocated} />
  <p>
    The older volume prints how much of its own data was imputed and the newer survey does not. It
    is worth saying plainly: a quarter of the 1990 year-built column is an estimate the Bureau made
    for a household that did not answer, and the only reason anybody knows is that the Bureau
    published a table about it.
  </p>
</Article>
